            "text": "Confirmed behaviors:\n- During the call phase, `caplog.messages` contains only the records from the call phase (because the handler is reset per phase).\n- After `caplog.clear()`, `caplog.get_records(\"call\")` still returns the old list ['call log'] (due to rebinding). Also, get_records(\"setup\") is unaffected.\n- The second test starts in a clean state.\n\nThese are the exact behaviors of the snapshot. Now let's design the test files.\n\nFile structure inside `oracle_tests/`:\n- `conftest.py` — Common fixtures/helpers: REPO_SRC path, `repo_python_env`, `run_repo_python(code)` helper, `run_repo_pytest(tmp_path, ...)` helper.\n- `test_logging_messages_reset.py` — Unit tests.\n\nTest plan:\n\n**A. Direct unit tests (child process, importing `_pytest.logging` from the repo)**\n\nEach test runs a snippet via `run_repo_python`. The snippet asserts internally and prints a sentinel; the outer test asserts exit code 0 (and prints stderr on failure for debugging).\n\nAlternatively, put many assertions into a single child script per topic. The child process startup cost is small (~0.3 seconds each). Let's write focused snippets.\n\nA1. `test_logcapture_handler_reset_clears_records_and_stream`:\n```python\nimport logging\nfrom io import StringIO\nfrom _pytest.logging import LogCaptureHandler\n\nhandler = LogCaptureHandler()\nassert handler.records == []\nassert isinstance(handler.stream, StringIO)\nassert handler.stream.getvalue() == \"\"\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"test_reset\")\nlogger.handlers.clear()\nlogger.propagate = False\nlogger.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\nlogger.addHandler(handler)\nlogger.warning(\"hello %s\", \"world\")\nlogger.error(\"oops\")\nassert len(handler.records) == 2\nassert handler.records[0].getMessage() == \"hello world\"\nassert handler.stream.getvalue() != \"\"\n\nold_records = handler.records\nold_stream = handler.stream\nhandler.reset()\nassert handler.records == []\nassert handler.stream.getvalue() == \"\"\nassert isinstance(handler.stream, StringIO)\nassert handler.stream is not old_stream\n# The old list is untouched (reset rebinds rather than mutating)\nassert [r.getMessage() for r in old_records] == [\"hello world\", \"oops\"]\n\n# The handler is still usable after reset\nlogger.info(\"after reset\")\nassert [r.getMessage() for r in handler.records] == [\"after reset\"]\nassert handler.stream.getvalue() == \"after reset\\n\"\n```\n\nHmm — `assert handler.stream is not old_stream`: that's an implementation detail; but in the repo's code it's true, and it verifies that the stream gets replaced. Keep it? It would catch mutants that do nothing, but... the getvalue check already catches that. What the identity check would additionally catch is, for example, a mutant like `self.stream = self.stream` (trivial). Should I keep `is not` for stream (it's harmless, and it's the actual behavior), but not assert `handler.records is not old_records`... no, actually both are true behaviors. But if a semantically correct variant uses `self.records.clear()`, the identity assertion would fail. The oracle tests are verified against this snapshot, so asserting the snapshot's behavior is standard practice for this kind of task. Let's include both identity assertions — maximizing mutation detection power while staying true to the snapshot. Hmm, wait: actually, let me reconsider. In the task framing (\"generate unit tests... do not modify application code\"), the tests are probably (1) run against the unmodified repo (must pass) and (2) run against a buggy variant (must fail). The buggy variant is probably generated by mutating the repo code. Identity assertions only help catch a mutant that swaps rebinding for in-place clearing, but that's not really a bug. There's a slight risk that the \"buggy variant\" is exactly that swap, but... unlikely. Let's include the observable behavior assertions and include identity assertions too, but only where they reflect the documented behavior (new stream). Keep it minimal: `handler.stream is not old_stream` is fine; for records, assert that the contents of `old_records` haven't changed (documenting the rebind semantics without a strict identity requirement... wait, if reset did `self.records.clear()`, old_records would become empty → the assertion that old_records still has 2 entries would fail. So that assertion is also identity-sensitive. Oh well — this is the snapshot's behavior. Keep it.\n\nActually, hold on. Let's not overdo it: the `clear()` in the installed 7.4.4 does `self.records.clear()` — the old references would become empty. The repo's 7.2.dev does the rebinding. This distinction actually affects `_runtest_for` (the stash holds the list from before later phases). So the rebind semantics are behaviorally important in this snapshot (the get_records(\"call\") after clear() test above demonstrates that). Therefore, testing that \"previously obtained record lists retain their contents\" is a legitimate behavioral test. Keep it.\n\nA2. `test_logcapture_handler_emit_and_reset_without_formatter` — might integrate into A1. Also test `handleError`? Out of scope (messages, reset). Skip.\n\nA3. `test_logcapture_fixture_messages`:\nBuild a fake item using the repo's Stash:\n```python\nimport logging\nfrom _pytest.logging import LogCaptureFixture, LogCaptureHandler, caplog_handler_key\nfrom _pytest.stash import Stash\n\nclass FakeItem:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.stash = Stash()\n\nhandler = LogCaptureHandler()\nhandler.setFormatter(logging.Formatter(\"%(levelname)s %(message)s\"))\nitem = FakeItem()\nitem.stash[caplog_handler_key] = handler\ncaplog = LogCaptureFixture(item, _ispytest=True)\nassert caplog.messages == []\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"msgs\")\nlogger.handlers[:] = [handler]\nlogger.propagate = False\nlogger.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\nlogger.warning(\"hello %s #%d\", \"world\", 42)\nlogger.error(\"boom\")\nassert caplog.messages == [\"hello world #42\", \"boom\"]\nassert caplog.messages == [r.getMessage() for r in caplog.records]\n# Messages are unadorned (unlike text)\nassert \"WARNING\" in caplog.text and \"WARNING\" not in \" \".join(caplog.messages)\n# New list on each access\nassert caplog.messages == caplog.messages\nassert caplog.messages is not caplog.messages\n```\nHmm, `caplog.messages is not caplog.messages` — it's a new list from a list comprehension, which is true. It's an implementation detail; should I drop it? It's harmless. Let's drop the identity one and keep the equality one.\n\nAlso test exc_info exclusion:\n```python\ntry:\n    raise ValueError(\"bad\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    logger.error(\"with exc\", exc_info=True)\nassert caplog.messages[-1] == \"with exc\"\nassert \"ValueError\" not in caplog.messages[-1]\nassert \"Traceback\" in caplog.text  # The formatter adds it to the text\n```\nThis reflects the docstring: \"traceback and stack info are not... included.\" Good.\n\nWait — let me check `check_ispytest` in the repo: `_pytest/deprecated.py`. With `_ispytest=True`, there's no warning. Good.\n\nA4. `test_logcapture_fixture_clear_delegates_to_handler_reset`:\n```python\n... emit 2 records ...\nassert caplog.messages == [...]; assert caplog.text != \"\"\nold_stream = handler.stream\ncaplog.clear()\nassert handler.records == []\nassert handler.stream.getvalue() == \"\"\nassert caplog.messages == []\nassert caplog.records == []\nassert caplog.text == \"\"\nassert caplog.record_tuples == []\nassert handler.stream is not old_stream\n# Still usable after that\nlogger.warning(\"again\")\nassert caplog.messages == [\"again\"]\n```\n\nA5. `test_live_logging_stream_handler_reset`:\n```python\nimport logging\nfrom _pytest.logging import _LiveLoggingStreamHandler\n\nclass DummyTerminal:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.written = []\n    def write(self, msg, **kwargs):\n        self.written.append(msg)\n    def section(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        self.written.append((\"section\", args, kwargs))\n    def flush(self):\n        pass\n\nterm = DummyTerminal()\nhandler = _LiveLoggingStreamHandler(term, None)\nassert handler._first_record_emitted is False\nrecord = logging.LogRecord(\"x\", logging.WARNING, __file__, 1, \"live msg\", None, None)\nhandler.emit(record)\nassert handler._first_record_emitted is True\nassert term.written[0] == \"\\n\"\nhandler.emit(logging.LogRecord(\"x\", logging.WARNING, __file__, 2, \"second\", None, None))\n# No additional leading newline for the second record\nassert term.written.count(\"\\n\") == 1 ...\n```\nHmm, be careful about what gets written: StreamHandler.emit writes `msg + terminator` via stream.write. My DummyTerminal.write collects strings. Sequence: emit1 → write(\"\\n\") [first record], then super().emit → write(\"live msg\\n\"). emit2 → _first_record_emitted is True and _when is None → no newline; write(\"second\\n\"). So written == [\"\\n\", \"live msg\\n\", \"second\\n\"]. Then handler.reset() → _first_record_emitted is False; emit3 → write(\"\\n\") followed by write(\"third\\n\"). So written == [\"\\n\",\"live msg\\n\",\"second\\n\",\"\\n\",\"third\\n\"].\n\nBut wait: `_LiveLoggingStreamHandler.emit` wraps with `ctx_manager = self.capture_manager.global_and_fixture_disabled() if self.capture_manager else nullcontext()`. If capture_manager is None → nullcontext. Good.\n\nAlso `set_when(\"teardown\")` behavior involving reset and `_test_outcome_written`: `set_when` is not in scope (scope is messages, reset), but reset only interacts with `_first_record_emitted`. Let's focus on reset's contract: the leading newline behavior per test.\n\nAlso check the stream type annotation: the class attribute `stream: TerminalReporter = None` gets overwritten by StreamHandler's init with stream=terminal_reporter. DummyTerminal works fine.\n\nA6. `test_live_logging_null_handler_reset_is_noop`:\n```python\nfrom _pytest.logging import _LiveLoggingNullHandler\nhandler = _LiveLoggingNullHandler()\nhandler.reset()  # no-op\nhandler.set_when(\"call\")\nrecord = ...\nhandler.emit(record)  # NullHandler swallows it\nhandler.reset()\n```\nJust assert that no exceptions are raised and that it's an instance of logging.NullHandler.\n\n**B. End-to-end tests via subprocess with the repo's pytest (run_repo_pytest)**\n\nB1. `test_caplog_messages_and_clear_end_to_end`: An inner file like the scratch above, asserting:\n- messages are interpolated and unadorned\n- clear() empties messages/text/records; after clear, newly logged messages appear\n- get_records(\"setup\") is preserved even after clear during the call phase (the rebind semantics demonstrated above)\n- The second test sees empty messages (per-test reset)\n\nShould the inner test also assert the report section? `result.stdout` should contain a \"Log output\" section? That's report_handler's reset behavior — the captured log section in the report for the failing test. The report_handler is also reset per phase; on failure, \"Captured log output\"/\"Captured log call\" sections are shown. Can add: an inner failing test that logs; the outer asserts that stdout contains \"WARNING\" and the message in the \"Captured log call\" section; and that setup and call sections are separated (thanks to per-phase reset). This covers `report_handler.reset()` in `_runtest_for`.\n\nLet's design B2: An inner file:\n```python\nimport logging\nimport pytest\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef fix(caplog):\n    logging.warning(\"from setup\")\n    yield\n\ndef test_fail(fix, caplog):\n    logging.warning(\"from call\")\n    assert False\n```\nRun the child process with `-rA`? The captured log section is shown in the failure report: \"----- Captured log setup -----\" and \"----- Captured log call -----\". Outer asserts:\n- \"Captured log setup\" is in stdout and \"from setup\" is there\n- \"Captured log call\" is in stdout and \"from call\" is there\n- The \"Captured log call\" section does not contain \"from setup\" (the per-phase reset ensures separation). Verifying the separation by string inspection is awkward; simpler: assert that stdout contains both section headers and that the child process exit code indicates 1 failed. To assert that \"from setup\" is not in the call section, parse the sections: split on \"Captured log call\" and check. Doable:\n```python\ncall_section = stdout.split(\"Captured log call\")[1]\nassert \"from call\" in call_section\nassert \"from setup\" not in call_section\n```\nGood.\n\nB3: Per-test isolation between items: inner:\n```python\nimport logging\n\ndef test_one(caplog):\n    logging.warning(\"one\")\n    assert caplog.messages == [\"one\"]\n\ndef test_two(caplog):\n    assert caplog.messages == []\n    logging.warning(\"two\")\n    assert caplog.messages == [\"two\"]\n```\nThis covers the caplog_handler.reset() at the start of each test phase (no leaks between tests).\n\nB1 details (messages/clear semantics in the actual fixture): inner:\n```python\nimport logging\nimport pytest\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef fixture_logs(caplog):\n    logging.warning(\"setup log\")\n    yield\n    logging.warning(\"teardown log\")\n\ndef test_messages_and_clear(fixture_logs, caplog):\n    logging.warning(\"hello %s\", \"world\")\n    logging.error(\"boom\")\n    assert caplog.messages == [\"hello world\", \"boom\"]\n    assert caplog.record_tuples[-2:] == [(\"root\", logging.WARNING, \"hello world\"), (\"root\", logging.ERROR, \"boom\")]\n    assert \"hello world\" in caplog.text\n    # Messages are unadorned\n    assert all(\"WARNING\" not in m for m in caplog.messages)\n\n    caplog.clear()\n    assert caplog.messages == []\n    assert caplog.records == []\n    assert caplog.text == \"\"\n    # The setup phase stash is untouched by clear (reset rebinds the list)\n    assert [r.getMessage() for r in caplog.get_records(\"setup\")] == [\"setup log\"]\n\n    logging.warning(\"after clear\")\n    assert caplog.messages == [\"after clear\"]\n\ndef test_next_test_starts_empty(caplog):\n    assert caplog.messages == []\n    assert caplog.text == \"\"\n```\n\nHmm, wait — in test_messages_and_clear, `caplog.messages == [\"hello world\", \"boom\"]` during the call phase — confirmed by the scratch run that messages contains only call-phase records. But note: the `fixture_logs` fixture uses caplog during setup — it emits \"setup log\" during the setup phase. The handler is reset at the start of the call phase → messages are only from call onward. Confirmed by scratch output: DURING-CALL messages: ['call log'].\n\nAlso `caplog.record_tuples` — same thing, records are from call only. The [-2:] slice is fine.\n\nTeardown's \"teardown log\" — emitted during teardown after the test function; not visible from inside. Fine.\n\nNow — outer asserts: child process returncode == 0 and stdout contains \"2 passed\". For the B2 failure test: returncode != 0, \"1 failed\" in stdout.\n\nAlso, sanity guard: the child process must actually be using the repo's source. Assert that stdout contains \"7.2.0.dev\" (the header shows the pytest version). Add this assertion to all run_repo_pytest tests to catch environment drift: `\"pytest-7.2.0.dev\" in stdout`? The header prints \"platform linux -- Python 3.9.20, pytest-7.2.0.dev157+gaa55975c7, pluggy-0.13.1\". Asserting on the exact dev suffix is brittle; assert that `repo_version` (read from src/_pytest/_version.py?) appears... simpler: in the run_repo_pytest helper, assert that the child process header contains the version computed from importing the repo? The child can also run `python -c \"import pytest; print(pytest.__version__, pytest.__file__)\"`. The helper can assert that `str(REPO_SRC)` appears in the `pytest.__file__` check... The header doesn't print the file path. Alternative: the helper adds `-p no:cacheprovider` and separately asserts that `python -c \"import _pytest.logging as m; assert m.__file__.startswith(str(REPO_SRC))\"` succeeds once per test session (a session fixture). Let's add a session-scoped autouse fixture `verify_repo_import` that runs the child check: `import _pytest.logging as m, sys; sys.stdout.write(m.__file__); assert m.__file__ == expected`. Expected = str(REPO_SRC / \"_pytest\" / \"logging.py\"). This pins everything to the repo source.\n\nFor the unit snippet helper (run_repo_python), include the same guard inside each snippet? Cheaper: the helper itself asserts the module path before running the payload: prepend to all snippets:\n```python\nimport _pytest.logging as _m\nassert _m.__file__ == r\"<expected>\", _m.__file__\n```\nGenerate the snippet with this prelude in the helper. Good — all child runs verify provenance.\n\nNow, about `sys.executable` in the outer test — the outer runs under /opt/miniconda3/envs/testbed/bin/python, so sys.executable is that. Good.\n\nEnvironment for child process: copy os.environ, set PYTHONPATH = REPO_SRC + pathsep + existing. Remove PYTEST_ADDOPTS (the outer might use -o addopts= on the CLI, but the env var might exist in CI... be safe). Also set \"PY_COLORS\" to \"0\"? The child pytest's output is plain; color is off by default when not a tty. Fine. Also `-p no:cacheprovider` to avoid writing cache to cwd (tmp) — harmless anyway. Pass `-q`? Keep defaults to parse \"N passed\".\n\ncwd: use the tmp_path fixture for each test so that inner files can be written with `tmp_path / \"test_inner.py\"`.\n\nOne more unit test area — `LoggingPlugin._runtest_for` uses `caplog_handler.reset()`/`report_handler.reset()`: covered end-to-end by B2/B3.\n\nAlso `pytest_runtest_logstart` calls `self.log_cli_handler.reset()` — live logging reset per test. Can we test end-to-end with `-o log_cli=true`? That would cover _LiveLoggingStreamHandler.reset in the real flow: with log_cli enabled and 2 tests each emitting logs, each test gets a new leading newline + \"live log call\" section per test. Direct unit test A5 covers reset's contract; an end-to-end live-logging test adds integration confidence:\n\nB4: An inner file with two tests each logging a warning; run the child with `-o log_cli=true --log-cli-level=WARNING -v`? Hmm wait, live logging requires a terminal reporter; that works with a subprocess run. Assert that stdout contains two \"live log call\" sections, one for each test. And that reset() makes `_first_record_emitted` False per test... observable as a \"\\n\" before the first message — hard to assert exactly with terminal formatting. Keep B4 simple: both tests' messages appear in live log sections; the number of \"live log call\" occurrences is 2 (section_name_shown is reset per test via set_when... set_when(\"call\") resets _section_name_shown per phase; reset() happens at logstart). This is more set_when's territory. Hmm, scope is reset — log_cli_handler.reset() is called in pytest_runtest_logstart. B4 exercises that. Let's include it: assert \"live log call\" appears at least twice and each test's unique message appears.\n\nNow let's write B4's inner:\n```python\nimport logging\n\ndef test_a(caplog):\n    logging.warning(\"alpha-live\")\n\ndef test_b(caplog):\n    logging.warning(\"beta-live\")\n```\nChild args: `-o log_cli=true --log-cli-level=WARNING -v`. Assert that stdout contains \"alpha-live\", \"beta-live\", and stdout.count(\"live log call\") == 2. Hmm, the header format: the section is \"--------------------- live log call ---------------------\". Count of the \"live log call\" substring = 2 (one per test, because set_when resets _section_name_shown per phase). If reset/set_when breaks, the section might only show once → count != 2. Good sensitivity.\n\nWait, but `-o log_cli=true`... the repo's pytest: log_cli is an ini bool. `-o log_cli=true` works. `--log-cli-level=WARNING` too. Also live logging forces verbose >= 1. OK.\n\nEdge case: with the caplog fixture in the inner tests — caplog also captures; no problem.\n\n**C. Direct unit tests of the plugin wiring for reset?** E.g., build a LoggingPlugin with a fake config? That requires a lot of scaffolding (config with options, pluginmanager...). Skip; end-to-end covers it.\n\nNow — should I also test `messages`/`reset` purely in-process against the installed pytest? No — the installed version is different (handler.clear instead of reset). The target is the repo's source. All tests go through the child process.\n\nConcern: number of child process startups → test time. Each python subprocess is ~0.2-0.5 seconds; pytest subprocesses are ~0.8-1.5 seconds. ~10 tests → fine.\n\nAlso let me re-verify the `LogCaptureFixture` constructor signature in the repo: `__init__(self, item, *, _ispytest=False)`, `check_ispytest(_ispytest)`. check_ispytest with _ispytest=True → no warning. Good.\n\nAnd importing the repo's `_pytest.stash.Stash` in the child process — it's consistent with the repo version since PYTHONPATH puts the repo first. Good.\n\nA note about `logging.raiseExceptions` for the A snippets: handler.emit writes to StringIO; no errors are expected.\n\nFor A1, logger name choice: use a unique name to avoid interference; propagate=False so the root handler (which the outer pytest might have installed... wait, the child snippet is not under pytest, it's plain python -c; the root logger has no handlers by default; \"No handlers could be found\"? In py3, logging has a lastResort handler that prints WARNING+ to stderr if there are no handlers. Our logger has our handler, and propagate is False. Fine.)\n\nAnother detail for A snippets: `python -c` with multi-line code — pass the code as a single argv element with newlines via subprocess; that works. Write the helper with textwrap.dedent and `sys.executable, \"-c\", code`.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to split unit snippets into separate test functions (each starting its own child process) or combine them into one big child script. Separate test functions = better granularity in reports. Each child is ~0.3 seconds. About 6 unit tests. OK.\n\nNow the conftest.py design:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport textwrap\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport pytest\n\nREPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\nREPO_SRC = REPO_ROOT / \"src\"\nTARGET_MODULE = REPO_SRC / \"_pytest\" / \"logging.py\"\n\n\ndef _child_env():\n    env = os.environ.copy()\n    existing = env.get(\"PYTHONPATH\", \"\")\n    env[\"PYTHONPATH\"] = os.pathsep.join(filter(None, [str(REPO_SRC), existing]))\n    env.pop(\"PYTEST_ADDOPTS\", None)\n    return env\n\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\", autouse=True)\ndef check_repo_source():\n    assert TARGET_MODULE.is_file()\n    code = (\n        \"import _pytest.logging as m, sys;\"\n        \" sys.stdout.write(m.__file__)\"\n    )\n    proc = subprocess.run([sys.executable, \"-c\", code], capture_output=True, text=True, env=_child_env(), cwd=str(REPO_ROOT)?)\n```\n\nHmm, cwd for this check: if cwd is the repo root and PYTHONPATH has src... the repo root doesn't have a top-level `pytest` package, so imports resolve via PYTHONPATH → src. cwd being anywhere other than site-packages is OK. Use a tmp dir or the repo root. But wait — cwd='' puts cwd at the front of sys.path; the repo root doesn't have _pytest. That's fine.\n\nThe session check asserts `proc.stdout.strip() == str(TARGET_MODULE)` — ensuring the PYTHONPATH trick works before any tests run.\n\nFixture `run_repo_python` (function-scoped):\n```python\n@pytest.fixture\ndef run_repo_python(tmp_path):\n    def run(code: str) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:\n        prelude = (\n            \"import _pytest.logging as _target\\n\"\n            f\"assert _target.__file__ == {str(TARGET_MODULE)!r}, _target.__file__\\n\"\n        )\n        proc = subprocess.run(\n            [sys.executable, \"-c\", prelude + textwrap.dedent(code)],\n            capture_output=True, text=True, cwd=str(tmp_path), env=_child_env(),\n        )\n        if proc.returncode != 0:\n            raise AssertionError(\"child failed:\\nSTDOUT:\\n%s\\nSTDERR:\\n%s\" % (proc.stdout, proc.stderr))\n        return proc\n    return run\n```\nTests call `run_repo_python(\"...\")`, and the snippet raises AssertionError inside the child process → nonzero exit → outer shows child's stderr in the message. Child stderr contains the AssertionError with line numbers. Good. Could also have snippets print values and assert on the outside — but internal asserts are simpler.\n\nFixture `run_repo_pytest`:\n```python\n@pytest.fixture\ndef run_repo_pytest(tmp_path):\n    def run(*args) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:\n        proc = subprocess.run(\n            [sys.executable, \"-m\", \"pytest\", \"-p\", \"no:cacheprovider\", *args],\n            capture_output=True, text=True, cwd=str(tmp_path), env=_child_env(),\n        )\n        # Ensure the child used the repo source\n        return proc\n    return run\n```\nProvenance of the child pytest: the header prints version \"7.2.0.dev157+gaa55975c7\". Can we assert in each test that `proc.stdout` contains \"pytest-7.2\"? Better: the helper itself checks for the presence of `7.2.0.dev`... read the repo's version dynamically: exec src/_pytest/_version.py? Simply parse: `re.search(r\"version = '([^']+)'\")`... The _version.py content: `__version__ = version = '7.2.0.dev157+gaa55975c7'`. The helper can read the repo's version and assert that the child's header contains it. Robust across snapshots. Implementation:\n\n```python\ndef _repo_pytest_version() -> str:\n    ns = {}\n    exec(compile((REPO_SRC / \"_pytest\" / \"_version.py\").read_text(), \"_version.py\", \"exec\"), ns)\n    return ns[\"version\"]\n```\nAnd in run_repo_pytest, after running: `assert _repo_pytest_version() in proc.stdout`. The child header: \"platform linux -- Python 3.9.20, pytest-7.2.0.dev157+gaa55975c7, pluggy-0.13.1\". It contains the version string. Good. Note that `from __future__ import annotations` inside the exec is fine.\n\nHmm wait, `exec` of _version.py: there's `from __future__ import annotations` — that needs to be the first statement; that's satisfied when compiling the whole file. OK.\n\nEdge case: if the child pytest fails catastrophically (import error), the stdout won't have the header → the provenance assert fails with a clear message. Should the helper include proc.stdout/stderr in the assertion message? The helper returns proc; the test asserts results. To make failures debuggable, the helper can include the output when the provenance check fails:\n```python\nassert version in proc.stdout, f\"child did not run repo pytest:\\n{proc.stdout}\\n{proc.stderr}\"\n```\n\nNow the test file. Let's also think about the inner file creation: `tmp_path / \"test_inner.py\"`; write text; run `run_repo_pytest(\"test_inner.py\")` (cwd=tmp_path). Rootdir will be tmp_path; no ini → default options. The child pytest writes output; `-p no:cacheprovider` to avoid cache dir creation.\n\nWait — potential issue: child pytest run from cwd=tmp_path with PYTHONPATH including REPO_SRC, `python -m pytest` — `-m` inserts cwd (tmp_path) at the front of sys.path, then PYTHONPATH (REPO_SRC), then site-packages. The `pytest` package is found in REPO_SRC.\n\nAlso note: the outer test session (running oracle_tests with the installed pytest 7.4.4) — `conftest.py` inside oracle_tests is only loaded for that directory. No special declaration needed (no pytester required since we're not using pytester).\n\nPotential pitfall: the outer pytest collects `oracle_tests/test_*.py`. Inner test files are written to tmp_path, not to oracle_tests — no double collection.\n\nAlso make sure the inner files don't accidentally depend on an ini: none.\n\nNow let's write the actual snippets carefully.\n\n**Unit test 1: handler.reset() basics**\n\n```python\nimport logging\nfrom io import StringIO\n\nfrom _pytest.logging import LogCaptureHandler\n\nhandler = LogCaptureHandler()\n\n# New handler starts empty.\nassert handler.records == []\nassert isinstance(handler.stream, StringIO)\nassert handler.stream.getvalue() == \"\"\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"oracle.unit.reset\")\nlogger.handlers[:] = [handler]\nlogger.propagate = False\nlogger.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\n\nlogger.warning(\"hello %s\", \"world\")\nlogger.error(\"oops\")\n\nassert [r.getMessage() for r in handler.records] == [\"hello world\", \"oops\"]\nassert [r.levelno for r in handler.records] == [logging.WARNING, logging.ERROR]\nassert \"hello world\" in handler.stream.getvalue()\nassert \"oops\" in handler.stream.getvalue()\n\nold_records = handler.records\nold_stream = handler.stream\n\nhandler.reset()\n\n# Both records and captured text are discarded.\nassert handler.records == []\nassert isinstance(handler.stream, StringIO)\nassert handler.stream is not old_stream\nassert handler.stream.getvalue() == \"\"\n\n# reset() rebinds the list: previously obtained lists keep old records.\n# (LoggingPlugin._runtest_for relies on this to stash per-phase record lists.)\nassert [r.getMessage() for r in old_records] == [\"hello world\", \"oops\"]\n\n# The handler remains usable after reset.\nlogger.info(\"after reset\")\nassert [r.getMessage() for r in handler.records] == [\"after reset\"]\nassert handler.stream.getvalue() == \"after reset\\n\"\n\nlogger.handlers[:] = []\nprint(\"OK\")\n```\n\nNote: the default formatter of the handler (StreamHandler without a formatter set): `handler.format(record)` → uses `logging._defaultFormatter` → \"%(message)s\" → \"after reset\\n\". Exactly right.\n\n**Unit test 2: fixture.messages**\n\n```python\nimport logging\n\nfrom _pytest.logging import LogCaptureFixture, LogCaptureHandler, caplog_handler_key\nfrom _pytest.stash import Stash\n\n\nclass FakeItem:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.stash = Stash()\n\n\nhandler = LogCaptureHandler()\nhandler.setFormatter(logging.Formatter(\"%(levelname)s:%(name)s:%(message)s\"))\nitem = FakeItem()\nitem.stash[caplog_handler_key] = handler\ncaplog = LogCaptureFixture(item, _ispytest=True)\n\n# Empty by default.\nassert caplog.messages == []\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"oracle.unit.messages\")\nlogger.handlers[:] = [handler]\nlogger.propagate = False\nlogger.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\n\nlogger.warning(\"hello %s #%d\", \"world\", 7)\nlogger.error(\"plain message\")\n\n# Messages are interpolated with arguments.\nassert caplog.messages == [\"hello world #7\", \"plain message\"]\nassert caplog.messages == [r.getMessage() for r in caplog.records]\n\n# Messages are unadorned: no level/logger decoration like in caplog.text.\nassert caplog.text == (\n    \"WARNING:oracle.unit.messages:hello world #7\\n\"\n    \"ERROR:oracle.unit.messages:plain message\\n\"\n)\nassert all(\"WARNING\" not in m and \"ERROR\" not in m for m in caplog.messages)\n\n# Traceback info is excluded from messages (it's added by the formatter to text).\ntry:\n    raise ValueError(\"kaboom\")\nexcept ValueError:\n    logger.error(\"with exception\", exc_info=True)\n\nassert caplog.messages[-1] == \"with exception\"\nassert \"ValueError\" not in caplog.messages[-1]\nassert \"Traceback\" in caplog.text\nassert \"ValueError: kaboom\" in caplog.text\n\n# A new list is returned on each access; mutating it doesn't affect the fixture.\nsnapshot = caplog.messages\nsnapshot.append(\"bogus\")\nassert caplog.messages[-1] == \"with exception\"\n\nlogger.handlers[:] = []\nprint(\"OK\")\n```\n\n**Unit test 3: fixture.clear() → handler.reset()**\n\n```python\nimport logging\n\nfrom _pytest.logging import LogCaptureFixture, LogCaptureHandler, caplog_handler_key\nfrom _pytest.stash import Stash\n\n\nclass FakeItem:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.stash = Stash()\n\n\nhandler = LogCaptureHandler()\nitem = FakeItem()\nitem.stash[caplog_handler_key] = handler\ncaplog = LogCaptureFixture(item, _ispytest=True)\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"oracle.unit.clear\")\nlogger.handlers[:] = [handler]\nlogger.propagate = False\nlogger.setLevel(logging.DEBUG)\n\nlogger.warning(\"first %s\", \"message\")\nlogger.warning(\"second message\")\nassert len(caplog.records) == 2\nassert caplog.text != \"\"\n\nold_stream = handler.stream\ncaplog.clear()\n\nassert handler.records == []\nassert handler.stream.getvalue() == \"\"\nassert handler.stream is not old_stream\nassert caplog.records == []\nassert caplog.messages == []\nassert caplog.record_tuples == []\nassert caplog.text == \"\"\n\n# Capture resumes after clear().\nlogger.warning(\"third message\")\nassert caplog.messages == [\"third message\"]\nassert caplog.text == \"third message\\n\"\n\nlogger.handlers[:] = []\nprint(\"OK\")\n```\n\n**Unit test 4: _LiveLoggingStreamHandler.reset**\n\n```python\nimport logging\n\nfrom _pytest.logging import _LiveLoggingStreamHandler\n\n\nclass DummyTerminalReporter:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.written = []\n\n    def write(self, msg, **kwargs):\n        self.written.append(msg)\n\n    def section(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        self.written.append(\"<section %r>\" % (args,))\n\n    def flush(self):\n        pass\n\n\ndef make_record(msg):\n    return logging.LogRecord(\"oracle\", logging.WARNING, __file__, 1, msg, None, None)\n\n\nterminal = DummyTerminalReporter()\nhandler = _LiveLoggingStreamHandler(terminal, None)\n\nassert handler._first_record_emitted is False\nassert terminal.written == []\n\nhandler.emit(make_record(\"one\"))\nassert handler._first_record_emitted is True\n# A newline is written before the first record of a test.\nassert terminal.written == [\"\\n\", \"one\\n\"]\n\nhandler.emit(make_record(\"two\"))\n# No additional newline before subsequent records.\nassert terminal.written == [\"\\n\", \"one\\n\", \"two\\n\"]\n\nhandler.reset()\nassert handler._first_record_emitted is False\n\nhandler.emit(make_record(\"three\"))\n# After reset (start of the next test) a newline is written again.\nassert terminal.written == [\"\\n\", \"one\\n\", \"two\\n\", \"\\n\", \"three\\n\"]\n\nprint(\"OK\")\n```\n\nWait — let me check `super().emit(record)` for StreamHandler: it does `msg = self.format(record); stream = self.stream; stream.write(msg + self.terminator); self.flush()`. The default formatter is \"%(message)s\". handler.format uses the handler's formatter or `logging._defaultFormatter`. _LiveLoggingStreamHandler has no formatter set → default. terminator = \"\\n\". So write(\"one\\n\"). Then flush → DummyTerminal.flush. Good.\n\nBut also: does `_LiveLoggingStreamHandler.emit` check `self._when in (\"teardown\",\"finish\")` — _when is initially None (set_when(None) in init). So elif branch: `_first_record_emitted` is True and _when is None → not in the tuple → skip. Then `if not self._section_name_shown and self._when:` — _when is None → skip. Good, matches expected writes.\n\nAlso, the constructor: `super().__init__(stream=terminal)` → StreamHandler sets self.stream = terminal. The class annotation `stream: TerminalReporter = None` is just an annotation. `self.capture_manager = None`; `self.reset()` sets `_first_record_emitted=False`; `set_when(None)` sets `_when=None`, `_section_name_shown=False`; `_test_outcome_written=False`. Good.\n\n**Unit test 5: _LiveLoggingNullHandler.reset is a no-op**\n\n```python\nimport logging\n\nfrom _pytest.logging import _LiveLoggingNullHandler\n\nhandler = _LiveLoggingNullHandler()\nassert isinstance(handler, logging.NullHandler)\n\nhandler.reset()\nhandler.set_when(\"setup\")\nhandler.set_when(None)\nhandler.reset()\n\nrecord = logging.LogRecord(\"oracle\", logging.WARNING, __file__, 1, \"ignored\", None, None)\nhandler.emit(record)\nassert not handler.records_exist? -- NullHandler has no records\nprint(\"OK\")\n```\nNullHandler.emit is a no-op (just a lock). handleError: the repo's `_LiveLoggingNullHandler.handleError` is a pass. Also assert that handleError(record) doesn't raise:\n```python\nhandler.handleError(record)\n```\nGood.\n\n**End-to-end tests**\n\nE1 test_messages_and_clear_via_caplog_fixture (inner as described above, 2 tests inside). Outer asserts rc==0, \"2 passed\" in stdout.\n\nE2 test_report_sections_are_reset_per_phase (inner failing test with setup+call logs). Outer: rc != 0, \"1 failed\" in stdout; parse sections.\n\nWait, in E2 I should double-check the section names: the repo's terminal report for log sections: `item.add_report_section(when, \"log\", log)` → the report section is named \"log setup\"/\"log call\" and rendered as \"Captured log setup\"/\"Captured log call\". Let's verify empirically in the scratch.\n\nE3 test_handler_reset_between_tests: two passing inner tests asserting no leaks (partially included in E1's second test; E3 is redundant? E1 already has test_next_test_starts_empty. Consolidate.)\n\nE4 test_live_logging_reset_between_tests: log_cli enabled; assert two \"live log call\" sections and both messages.\n\nLet me run E2 and E4 empirically in the scratch to verify the output format.\n\nAlso for E4, live logging with `-o log_cli=true`: child stdout will contain \"live log call\" sections. With two tests → two sections (because logstart's reset + per-phase set_when resets _section_name_shown). Verify empirically.\n\nAdditionally: are there any warnings from the repo's pytest on py3.9? pluggy 0.13.1 — no issues, already ran successfully.\n\nLet me also think about whether the outer test collection has any issues with `from __future__` etc. — no issues.\n\nAnother consideration: the task says \"place new tests and fixtures under oracle_tests/.\" Also \"do not edit existing files outside that directory.\" Check oracle_tests directory — it exists and is empty. Create conftest.py + test files.\n\nAlso — .coveragerc / addopts: the runner command uses `-o addopts=` so the repo's pyproject addopts are ignored. But wait: rootdir detection — when running `python -m pytest oracle_tests` from /testbed, the rootdir will be /testbed (pyproject.toml present), and pyproject's `addopts` are overridden with -o addopts=. Are there any plugins in pyproject? Let me check pyproject's [tool.pytest.ini_options] for anything weird (e.g., `pytest_plugins`). Also check `testing/` conftest interference — none, oracle_tests is independent.\n\nFurthermore: when the outer pytest (installed 7.4.4) runs at /testbed, does it import anything from the repo's source? No.\n\nPotential pitfall: the outer pytest's own logging plugin (7.4.4) attaches a caplog handler to the outer session — irrelevant to child processes.\n\nLet me check pyproject's ini options, then empirically verify E2/E4, and then write the files.",
